{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Narration moved out of the description to keep it under the plan-review admission cap (8000 chars). Preserved here verbatim so nothing is lost.\n\nSuperseded \"Likely cause\" guess, and why it was wrong:\n\n> The first version of the root-cause section guessed that a field \"is represented as a JS `BigInt` somewhere in the MCP server's serialization path\". That was wrong. It was written by the GitHub Copilot CLI session that hit the error, which attributed a JavaScript error to a Python server. rebar's MCP server is Python (FastMCP + pydantic); it has no `BigInt` type and never calls `JSON.stringify`. The throw is in the JS client, but the cause is on our side: the server put `time.time_ns()` timestamps on the wire as bare 19-digit JSON numbers.\n\nAlso trimmed from the description (unchanged in substance, still true):\n\n- The tools that do NOT fail are exactly those whose models carry no ticket timestamps — `search`, `summary`, `ticket_deps`, `explain_criterion`, `grounding_info` — which emit 0 out-of-range integers. That split predicted precisely which tools break, before the fix.\n- Copilot CLI's own contributing bug is a lossless parse followed by a naive `JSON.stringify` with no BigInt replacer. Not rebar's to fix, and not the root cause: it does not explain the silent truncation seen in the other client."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787891240913335044,"uuid":"0009f497-2202-41c8-ad1b-8e557e5e25b3"}